From 96750f22e4397d4bb4cc366fe3721ed85ba41da8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 02:31:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gbm: setting for the brightness of the GUI and text subtitles
 in HDR

While the output is HDR (PQ or HLG, and native Dolby Vision, which composites the
GUI as PQ), the GUI is composited with GUI white at the BT.2408 reference white,
203 nits, fixed. Kodi's setting for it (videoscreen.guipeakluminance) exists only
on Windows, Android and webOS, and its scale would not give 203 nits at its
default.

videoscreen.hadesguiwhite sets GUI white from 25 to 300 % of the reference
white (51 to 609 nits in PQ), 100 % by default. It covers everything the GUI pass
draws: menus, OSD, text subtitles and bitmap subtitles of SDR origin. Bitmap
subtitles authored in PQ (UHD Blu-ray PGS) are drawn with their own palette in
another pass and keep their levels; the video is never touched.

Only LUT data changes, the shader does not:
- PQ: the TF LUT covers [0, white * 203 nits];
- HLG: the degamma LUT is scaled, as the shader maps linear 1.0 to 203 nits on the
  1000-nit reference display. Up to 300 % no colour reaches the top of the HLG
  signal (saturated blue would from about 335 %, through the inverse OOTF).
At 100 % both LUTs are computed exactly as before (a factor of exactly 1.0f), so
the output is bit for bit Kodi's.

CreateLUTs() now builds the new textures aside and replaces the ones in use only
when all of them exist, so a change applied live from BeginGuiComposite() keeps a
working GUI if it fails; a failed value is tried again once the setting has gone
back to the value in use.

The setting requires GBM with GLES (the GL context composites with its own fixed
white) and shows with an HDR display, in the HDR group of the display settings.
Its label and help are the English core strings 39997 and 39998, translated in
Estuary (31690, 31691: es_es, es_ar, es_mx) and used with Estuary as the skin
(CGUIUtils::GetHadesString()).
